      "writtenOn": "2024-05-11T01:13:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The async code requires some extra threads and has a few bugs open that will be hard to fix if there are more users. Are we sure we want to use it more?",
      "revId": "d0578488dad8206fb3ef16af0b51ea4ccfdf06df",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "80f1e28c_51443b91",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 2009465
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-05-13T08:35:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Since the library is vendor_available, it can already be used by the OEMs on the /vendor partition. Exposing it to /product should not be a major change in the availability of the library - it only gives the OEMs (and their dependencies) another option for where to use it.",
      "parentUuid": "d455a306_3cdbce90",
      "revId": "d0578488dad8206fb3ef16af0b51ea4ccfdf06df",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b8150c84_e4419243",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1892347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-05-16T13:06:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I do want to fix b/205673378 eventually, but last time I looked at it, I found it to be rather non-trivial and significantly more work than I had expected. It would require the AIDL generator to generate two copies of anything that contains a binder node, which pretty much all of the things that it generates can do.\n\nI want to get around to it eventually, but right now I need to prioritize some Rust Binder driver work. I wonder if we could add another option to the AIDL generator (like how generating async at all is already an option) in a way that makes it easy to transition users one-by-one when I fix the bug?\n\n(Actually transitioning users shouldn\u0027t be difficult — it\u0027s just removing / adding calls to `into_async()` or `into_sync()`. The difficulty is that right now, we would need to update all users simultaneously as the change could otherwise break users.)\n\nYou say \"bugs\" in plural. Are there any other bugs you are worried about?",
      "parentUuid": "80f1e28c_51443b91",
      "revId": "d0578488dad8206fb3ef16af0b51ea4ccfdf06df",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "50cdafbd_8760c663",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-05-17T20:51:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For that bug, we can close it then, if it only needs into_async/into_sync. Seems okay for WAI, if you want now.\n\nb/293406123 though is the other one I think could be an issue?",
      "parentUuid": "b8150c84_e4419243",
      "revId": "d0578488dad8206fb3ef16af0b51ea4ccfdf06df",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "df750384_fbe87711",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1892347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-05-17T21:21:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Okay, then I am closing it.\n\nAs for b/293406123, I\u0027m a bit confused. The kernel driver does not support local calls at all, and aosp/2674857 introduced a test that goes through the relevant codepath. So perhaps it\u0027s not actually a bug at all? Whatever the code does, it does not go through the kernel driver.\n\nEither way, b/293406123 doesn\u0027t seem like it has issues with future-compat, since we would not need to change the API to fix it (if it\u0027s a real problem).",
      "parentUuid": "50cdafbd_8760c663",
      "revId": "d0578488dad8206fb3ef16af0b51ea4ccfdf06df",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "59bd806c_887fa071",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-05-17T22:01:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Normally, if you have a binder method like:\n\n```\nclass IFoo { void doSomething(...); }\n```\n\nAnd you have a local instance of it, if you call doSomething, it will directly dispatch to the local instance in-process, without serializing.\n\nThe question I think is, if you have async local client, and sync local server (or vice versa), then will it dispatch a direct call of the implementation when you call the proxy code, whether that has to get executed in the coroutine context or not? The other way this could work without the binder driver is by serializing the call in-process and then dispatching the call in process. If this happens, it would be a waste to serialize it, and we should fix it.\n\nI\u0027m not sure what implications this has on the async API.",
      "parentUuid": "df750384_fbe87711",
      "revId": "d0578488dad8206fb3ef16af0b51ea4ccfdf06df",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
